objective:
Add recurring orders to stockroom: templates that place the same order again and again on a schedule (every N days, or on fixed weekdays), a generator that adds the orders that are due to an order book without ever placing one twice, a CSV format for templates, and a `recur` command that can save the orders file.

Create the module `stockroom/recurring.py` with:

- `WEEKDAYS = ("mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri", "sat", "sun")` (index 0 is Monday, as `date.weekday()` counts);
- `Template`, a frozen dataclass with the fields `template_id` (str), `customer` (str), `start` (`datetime.date`), `end` (`datetime.date | None`), `every_days` (`int | None`), `weekdays` (tuple of int) and `lines` (tuple of `OrderLine`), in that order;
- `occurrences(template: Template, until: date) -> list[tuple[int, date]]`;
- `generate(templates: Sequence[Template], book: OrderBook, until: date, *, skip: Collection[date] = ()) -> list[Order]`;
- `TEMPLATE_HEADER = ("template_id", "customer", "start", "end", "every", "weekdays", "sku", "quantity", "unit_price")` and `read_templates(text: str) -> list[Template]`.

**Template** checks itself when it is created and raises `ParseError` when `template_id` is empty or only whitespace; when not exactly one schedule is given (either `every_days` is a number and `weekdays` is empty, or `every_days` is `None` and `weekdays` is not empty); when `every_days` is below 1; when `weekdays` holds a value outside 0-6 or is not strictly ascending (so no repeats either); when `lines` is empty; or when `end` is not after `start`. A template places orders from `start` (included) up to `end` (excluded; `None` means it never ends).

**occurrences(template, until)** lists the days on which the template places an order, up to `until` (included), as `(number, day)` pairs in date order. With `every_days`, the occurrence days are `start`, `start + every_days` days, `start + 2 * every_days` days, and so on; with `weekdays`, they are the days from `start` on (`start` itself included when its weekday fits) whose `date.weekday()` is in `weekdays`. The `number` of an occurrence is the 1-based count of the template's occurrence days from `start` up to and including that day. Only days before `end` (when there is one) and not after `until` are listed; an `until` before `start` gives `[]`.

**generate(templates, book, until, skip=())** places the orders that are due up to `until`. If two templates have the same `template_id`, it raises `StockroomError` before anything is added. For every template and every pair `(number, day)` of `occurrences(template, until)`, the order id is `<template_id>-<number>`. Nothing is placed for that occurrence when `day` is in `skip` (its number stays used: the following occurrences keep their own numbers) or when `book` already has an order with that id, whatever that order looks like (so calling `generate` again never places an order twice). Every other occurrence becomes `Order(order_id, template.customer, day, template.lines, "open")`. The new orders are sorted by `placed`, and orders placed on the same day by the position of their template in `templates`; they are added to `book` with `book.add` in that order and returned as a list in that order. `skip` only affects the call it is given to: a later call without it places the skipped occurrences.

**read_templates(text)**: the text is read with the `csv` module; rows whose cells are all empty or whitespace are skipped; the first remaining row is the header and its cells, with surrounding whitespace removed, must equal `TEMPLATE_HEADER`; every further row has exactly nine cells, each stripped of surrounding whitespace, and describes one line of a template. All rows of one template (same `template_id`, also when they are not adjacent) must agree on `customer` (as text), `start` and `end` (as dates), and `every` and `weekdays` (as read below). `start` is a `YYYY-MM-DD` date read like the other dates of the package; an empty `end` gives `None`, any other `end` is such a date; an empty `every` gives `None`, any other `every` must be written with the ASCII digits 0-9 only and is read with `int()`; `weekdays` is a list of names from `WEEKDAYS` (exactly those lower-case names) separated by whitespace, without repeats and in any order, read as the ascending tuple of their indexes (an empty cell gives `()`); `sku`, `quantity` (read with `int()`) and `unit_price` (read with `money.parse_money`) make an `OrderLine`. The templates come back in the order of their first rows, with their lines in file order, built as `Template` objects (so the template checks apply). Every problem raises `ParseError`.

**Command.** Add `python -m stockroom recur --templates TEMPLATES.csv --orders ORDERS.csv --until YYYY-MM-DD [--skip YYYY-MM-DD]... [--write]`. It reads `--until` and every `--skip` (the option may be repeated) like the other dates, the templates with `read_templates` and the orders file like `sales` does into an `OrderBook`, and calls `generate`. It prints, with exit code 0, one line per new order, in the returned order: `<order_id>  <customer>  <placed as YYYY-MM-DD>  <plural(units, "unit")>  <subtotal>`, where the units are the sum of the order's line quantities, `plural` is `textutil.plural`, and the subtotal (`pricing.order_subtotal`) is formatted as `money.format_money` does with the `currency_symbol` setting; then `<plural(n, "new order")>` for the number `n` of new orders (`0 new orders`, `1 new order`, `4 new orders`). Without `--write` the orders file is not changed. With `--write` the command then writes the orders file again, UTF-8, with the text that `csvio.write_orders` returns for the orders of the book in book order (the orders of the file first, then the new ones in the returned order), and prints `wrote <plural(m, "order")>` for the number `m` of orders in the book. Any error (a bad date, a bad file, duplicate template ids) ends the command with exit code 1, `stockroom: error: ...` on stderr and nothing on stdout, and leaves the orders file unchanged.
